                    round_name_r1:          `Diagnose`,
                    round_name_r2:          `Design & Budget`,
                    round_name_r3:          `Allocate Coaching`,
                    round_name_r4:          `Prove & Sustain`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the complexity gap`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `A pre-program assessment scores the 120 senior leaders at <strong>2.4 / 5</strong> on navigating ambiguity and competing demands, against a board target of <strong>3.6 / 5</strong>. Move past “they need leadership training.” The Confluence leaders <em>had</em> the techniques and still escalated — so this is a <strong>developmental</strong> gap (how leaders construe and hold complexity), not merely a skills one. Name the capacities most missing — cognitive (holding competing frames) and emotional (staying regulated under the anxiety) — and decide who needs what, rather than one-size-fits-all. Then define the behaviour you must see change to call this a success.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Select interventions within the budget`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Design the program inside a hard <strong>A$2.4M</strong> cap (~A$20,000/leader) with <em>no contingency</em>. The 70-20-10 logic says capability grows mostly through challenging experience (70%), then relationships like coaching (20%), then formal content (10%) — so weight action-learning and coaching over workshops. The prestige external AMP costs <strong>A$38,000/leader</strong>: routing the whole cohort through it is A$4.56M — 90% over budget. Resolve <strong>breadth</strong> (a thin layer for all 120) versus <strong>depth</strong> (intensive growth for a priority segment), and watch the time-off-the-job load against a transformation already running hot.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Allocate coaching under scarcity`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Executive coaching is the highest-impact, highest-cost element — a <strong>6-session, A$9,000</strong> engagement that changes behaviour — and the pool covers only <strong>40 of 120</strong> leaders at that depth. Define an explicit allocation rule (business-critical and the Confluence strugglers? highest-potential?), and choose <strong>depth-for-few</strong> over diluting it to a single session each, because <em>one session changes no one</em>. Extend reach with affordable peer-coaching circles. Then a sponsoring line executive lobbies for <em>their</em> three people regardless of the rule — hold the line, or your allocation becomes politics.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Prove it & sustain it`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present to the board sub-committee. Show how the program moves readiness toward <strong>3.6/5</strong> and define what the 12-month read-out actually measures: <strong>observable behaviour on live trade-offs</strong>, rated by managers and re-assessed against baseline — <em>not happy-sheets and self-report</em>. Connect leading indicators (action-learning engagement, coaching uptake) to lagging outcomes (behaviour on real decisions), and specify a <strong>sustain mechanism</strong> — manager reinforcement and embedding the capability in real decisions — so it doesn't fade by month 18. Then deliver the pitch.`,

                    r1_nature_label:        `How do you name the gap behind Confluence?`,
                    r1_capacity_label:      `Which complexity capacity is most missing?`,
                    r1_segment_label:       `How do you treat the 120 senior leaders?`,
                    r1_target_label:        `Sharpness of the success behaviour you must see change (%)`,
                    r1_target_readout:      `Defined as observable behaviour on live trade-offs:`,
                    nature_developmental:   `A developmental gap — they could not yet hold contradiction and stay regulated under it; this is capacity, not a slide deck`,
                    nature_skills:          `A skills gap — the leaders lacked techniques for leading through ambiguity; buy them the training`,
                    nature_motivation:      `A motivation/accountability gap — they had the skills but didn't take ownership; fix it with incentives`,
                    cap_both:               `Both cognitive (holding competing frames) AND emotional (staying regulated amid the anxiety) — Confluence failed on both`,
                    cap_cognitive:          `Mainly cognitive — they couldn't hold the regulatory-vs-community frames at once`,
                    cap_technical:          `Mainly technical depth — give them more domain expertise on regulation and community relations`,
                    seg_segmented:          `Segment them — a priority group near live trade-offs goes deep; the rest get a lighter, targeted layer`,
                    seg_uniform:            `One-size-fits-all — every leader gets the same program; it's the fairest design`,

                    r2_alloc_label:         `Build the intervention mix — A$M per lever across the cohort (must total ≤ A$2.40M)`,
                    r2_total_readout:       `Program spend:`,
                    r2_total_cap:           `/ A$2.40M cap`,
                    r2_balance_label:       `Resolve breadth vs. depth.`,
                    alloc_action:           `Action-learning on live transformation problems — the 70% (challenging experience)`,
                    alloc_coaching:         `Coaching pool (executive + peer circles) — the 20% (relationships)`,
                    alloc_workshops:        `Cohort workshops, 360 & assessment — the 10% (formal learning)`,
                    alloc_amp:              `External AMP at A$38k/leader — prestige open-enrolment program`,
                    bal_depth:              `Depth — concentrate intensive development on a priority segment; lighter touch for the rest`,
                    bal_breadth:            `Breadth — a thin, even layer across all 120 so no one is left out`,
                    bal_prestige:           `Prestige — route as many leaders as possible through the external AMP for the brand on the CV`,

                    r3_rule_label:          `Define the executive-coaching allocation rule (6 sessions, A$9k/leader; pool covers only 40 of 120).`,
                    r3_depth_label:         `Coaching depth posture.`,
                    r3_peer_label:          `Peer-coaching circles to extend reach to the other 80 (A$M)`,
                    r3_peer_readout:        `Spend on the peer-coaching layer:`,
                    r3_peer_unit:           `A$M`,
                    r3_peer_hint:           `Peer circles are cheap and scale, but they extend reach — they do not replace the depth that executive coaching buys for the priority few.`,
                    r3_lobby_label:         `The sponsoring line executive lobbies hard: “Give MY three people the coaching slots, rule or no rule.” You:`,
                    rule_critical:          `Most business-critical & the Confluence strugglers — where behaviour change moves live trade-offs now`,
                    rule_potential:         `Highest-potential talent — invest in the future executive bench`,
                    rule_equal:             `Equal — split the budget so every leader gets a session or two; it's the fair thing`,
                    rule_sponsor:           `Whoever their sponsoring executive nominates — keep the line leaders happy`,
                    depth_full:             `Depth-for-few — full 6-session engagements for ~40 leaders at behaviour-changing depth`,
                    depth_dilute:           `Diluted-for-many — stretch the pool to 120 leaders at 1–2 sessions each so it feels fair`,
                    lobby_hold:             `Hold the rule — explain it, apply it to their people on the same criteria as everyone else`,
                    lobby_transparent:      `Change it transparently — if the rule should flex, revise it openly for all sponsors, not in secret for one`,
                    lobby_cave:             `Cave — hand their three the scarce slots off-rule to keep the relationship`,

                    r4_measure_label:       `What does the 12-month read-out actually measure?`,
                    r4_indicators_label:    `How do you connect leading to lagging indicators?`,
                    r4_sustain_label:       `What sustains the capability after the program ends?`,
                    r4_pitch_label:         `Conviction in the board pitch: how firmly do you defend transfer over satisfaction? (%)`,
                    meas_behaviour:         `Observable behaviour on live trade-offs — rated by managers/peers, plus re-assessed complexity-readiness vs baseline`,
                    meas_completion:        `Completion & attendance — who finished the program and how many sessions they logged`,
                    meas_satisfaction:      `Satisfaction & self-report — happy-sheet scores and a self-rated confidence survey`,
                    ind_linked:             `Link them — leading signals (engagement, uptake) as an early read, validated against lagging behaviour on real decisions`,
                    ind_lagging:            `Wait for the lagging outcome only — measure behaviour change at month 12, nothing before`,
                    ind_leading:            `Track only leading signals — action-learning engagement and coaching uptake; assume outcomes follow`,
                    sustain_embed:          `Embed it — manager reinforcement and using the capability on real decisions, so growth continues after the coaches leave`,
                    sustain_refresh:        `An annual refresher workshop — bring the cohort back once a year`,
                    sustain_none:           `Nothing formal — a strong 12-month program should stick on its own`,

                    warn_target_range:      `The success-behaviour sharpness must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_alloc_negative:    `Intervention allocations cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_alloc_empty:       `You must allocate something to the program.`,
                    warn_alloc_over:        `That exceeds the A$2.4M cap — there is no contingency. You can submit it, but the board will see the over-spend.`,
                    warn_peer_value:        `The peer-coaching spend cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_pitch_range:       `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_developmental: `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> The Confluence leaders had the techniques and still escalated — so this is a developmental capacity to hold contradiction and stay regulated, not a missing slide deck. Now design something that grows <em>how</em> a leader holds a trade-off, not just the vocabulary for it.`,
                    coach_r1_skills:        `<strong>You've called it a skills gap and you'll buy more workshops.</strong> But your own evidence says these leaders knew the techniques and escalated anyway. Floris's point is that handling complexity is a developmental capacity, not a course. A skills framing here builds the wrong program.`,
                    coach_r1_motivation:    `<strong>It isn't that they wouldn't — it's that they couldn't yet.</strong> Re-framing a developmental limit as a motivation problem leads you to incentives instead of growth. They didn't lack will at Confluence; they lacked the capacity to hold the regulatory-vs-community tension.`,
                    coach_r1_technical:     `<strong>More domain depth misses it.</strong> These are technically outstanding leaders — that is exactly why they were promoted. The gap is holding competing frames <em>and</em> the anxiety they generate, not knowing more about regulation.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> A developmental gap on <em>both</em> cognitive and emotional complexity is precisely what Confluence revealed — they couldn't hold the regulatory-vs-community frame and tolerate the discomfort of owning it. That naming tells you what the program actually has to grow.`,
                    coach_r1_uniform:       `<strong>One-size-fits-all spreads the same average over very different leaders.</strong> A 25-year operator near a live regulatory trade-off needs something different from a newly-promoted director. Segment the 120, or you'll under-serve the people whose behaviour the board actually wants changed.`,
                    coach_r1_vaguetarget:   `<strong>A vague success behaviour can't be proven in 12 months.</strong> “Better leaders” isn't measurable. Define it as observable behaviour on a live trade-off — holding a tension instead of escalating it — or the read-out will be soft.`,
                    coach_r2_depth:         `<strong>Depth on a priority segment is the defensible call here.</strong> A transformation needs senior leaders to act differently in the next 12 months, and intensive development for the people nearest the live trade-offs moves behaviour faster than a thin layer for everyone. Now make sure the time-off-the-job load is bearable.`,
                    coach_r2_breadth:       `<strong>Breadth feels fair but rarely changes behaviour.</strong> A thin, even layer across 120 leaders gives everyone a little and no one enough to hold complexity differently. What did the leaders nearest the live trade-offs lose by being averaged in with the rest?`,
                    coach_r2_amp:           `<strong>You've routed the cohort through the A$38,000 AMP.</strong> 120 leaders at A$38k is A$4.56M against a A$2.4M budget — 90% over, with no contingency. Either you cover everyone shallowly or you go deep with some. Which is it, and why is that right for a transformation that needs different behaviour in 12 months?`,
                    coach_r2_overbudget:    `<strong>You're over the A$2.4M cap with no contingency.</strong> There is no rescue line — every dollar over comes out of credibility with a board that set a hard budget. Re-balance the mix until it lives inside A$2.4M.`,
                    coach_r2_contentheavy:  `<strong>Your mix inverts the 70-20-10 logic.</strong> You've weighted workshops and the AMP over action-learning and coaching — formal content over challenging experience and relationships. Capability grows mostly on the job; justify the inversion or re-weight toward the 70 and the 20.`,
                    coach_r3_critical:      `<strong>Targeting the business-critical and the Confluence strugglers is a defensible rule.</strong> Coaching where behaviour change moves a live trade-off now is exactly how a scarce, high-impact resource should be allocated. Can you read that rule aloud to the 80 who won't get deep coaching?`,
                    coach_r3_potential:     `<strong>High-potential is a real rule — name its cost.</strong> Investing in the future bench is legitimate, but it may not move the live trade-offs the board is worried about this year. Be explicit about why future potential beats present criticality.`,
                    coach_r3_equal:         `<strong>You gave every leader a session so “it's fair.”</strong> One session changes no one's behaviour — your own evidence says depth needs six. You've spent the coaching budget and moved nobody. Fair and effective aren't the same thing. What's your rule?`,
                    coach_r3_sponsorrule:   `<strong>“Whoever the sponsor nominates” is not an allocation rule — it's a queue for lobbying.</strong> The moment one sponsor's people get the slots, every other sponsor lobbies too, and your scarce coaching is just politics.`,
                    coach_r3_diluted:       `<strong>Diluting the pool to 120 at one session each spends everything and changes no one.</strong> Six sessions is the depth that changes behaviour; one is a taster. Depth-for-few beats diluted-for-many when the resource is this scarce.`,
                    coach_r3_peerover:      `<strong>Peer circles extend reach — they don't replace depth.</strong> Over-loading the peer layer at the expense of real executive coaching leaves your priority leaders without the engagement that actually changes behaviour. Keep peer coaching as the extension, not the core.`,
                    coach_r3_hold:          `<strong>You held the rule under pressure — that's the whole point.</strong> Explaining it and applying the same criteria to the sponsor's people is what keeps allocation a principle rather than a negotiation. A rule that survives lobbying is a rule; one that doesn't is just politics.`,
                    coach_r3_caved:         `<strong>You gave the sponsor's three people the scarce slots off-rule.</strong> Now every other sponsor will lobby too, and your allocation is just politics. Either your rule survives pressure or you don't have a rule — how would you hold it, or change it transparently for everyone?`,
                    coach_r4_behaviour:     `<strong>That's the read-out the board asked for.</strong> Observable behaviour on live trade-offs, rated by managers and re-assessed against the 2.4 baseline, evidences whether leaders now <em>hold</em> complexity they used to escalate — not whether they enjoyed the program.`,
                    coach_r4_completion:    `<strong>Completion and attendance prove people showed up, not that they changed.</strong> A leader can finish every session and still escalate the next hard trade-off. The board asked whether behaviour changed — measure that.`,
                    coach_r4_satisfaction:  `<strong>Happy-sheets and a self-rated survey aren't evidence of transfer.</strong> A leader can love the program and still escalate the next hard decision. The board asked whether they now hold complexity they used to escalate — what observable behaviour will you put in front of them?`,
                    coach_r4_leadingonly:   `<strong>Leading signals alone can mislead.</strong> High action-learning engagement and coaching uptake are encouraging, but they're inputs, not proof of behaviour change. Validate them against lagging behaviour on real decisions, or you're measuring activity.`,
                    coach_r4_nosustain:     `<strong>A beautiful 12-month program that then ends will fade by month 18.</strong> You measured transfer well — but development that isn't reinforced in real decisions decays. What keeps these leaders holding complexity once the coaches leave and the cohorts disband?`,
                    coach_r4_landed:        `<strong>That lands the whole program.</strong> A transfer-based read-out plus an embedding mechanism — manager reinforcement and using the capability on live decisions — is what proves capability rose and keeps it alive after the budget and the coaches are gone. That's the board pitch.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Developmental gap, both capacities named, cohort segmented.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Diagnosed as skills/motivation or treated the cohort as one.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `70-20-10-weighted mix, depth, inside the A$2.4M cap.`,
