                    sum_r3_good:            `Depth-for-few under a clear rule, line held.`,
                    sum_r3_diluted:         `Diluted coaching — spent the pool, moved no one.`,
                    sum_r3_caved:           `Caved to the sponsor — allocation became politics.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Coaching allocation under-defined or under-defended.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Transfer-based read-out, leading-to-lagging, embedded.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Measured satisfaction or left it un-sustained.`,

                    insight_built:          `Capability built, board convinced: you diagnosed a developmental gap, designed a 70-20-10-weighted program inside the A$2.4M cap, gave depth-for-few under a rule you held, and proved it with behaviour on live trade-offs plus an embedding mechanism. Readiness moved toward 3.6/5 with credible, observable evidence.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `You broke the A$2.4M budget. A board that set a hard, no-contingency cap reads an over-spend as a failure to live inside the constraint — the prestige AMP for everyone was the classic trap, and it cost you the breadth-vs-depth discipline.`,
                    insight_satisfaction:   `You measured satisfaction, not transfer. A 12-month read-out built on happy-sheets and self-report can't show the board whether leaders now hold complexity they used to escalate — and a leader can love the program and still escalate the next hard trade-off.`,
                    insight_short:          `You moved readiness partway but didn't fully close the gap. The design, the coaching allocation and the measurement still tell slightly different stories — enough that the board's 12-month read-out reads as progress rather than proof.`,
                    insight_diluted:        `You diluted the coaching to feel fair. One or two sessions each spends the scarce pool and changes no one's behaviour — six sessions is the depth that moves a leader. Depth-for-few would have moved more behaviour with the same money.`,
                    insight_caved:          `You caved to the sponsoring executive's lobbying. Handing the scarce slots to one sponsor's people off-rule turned your allocation into politics — and every other sponsor now has a precedent to lobby against.`,
                    insight_time:           `You over-ran the time-off-the-job envelope. A transformation already running hot resists pulling senior leaders away — too many development days off the job erodes the business's tolerance and the board's confidence.`,
                    insight_uniform:        `You treated the 120 leaders as one. A one-size-fits-all design under-serves the people nearest the live trade-offs — the very leaders whose behaviour the board most wants to see change.`,
                    insight_transfer:       `The capability change is evidenced in observable behaviour, not satisfaction — and embedded so it persists. That closed the gap between a program people enjoyed and one the board can trust 18 months on.`,

                    hint_budget:            `You're over the A$2.4M cap. Pull back the external AMP and re-weight toward action-learning and coaching — there is no contingency line to rescue an over-spend.`,
                    hint_caved:             `You handed scarce coaching to a lobbying sponsor off-rule. Re-anchor on an explicit allocation rule and apply it to everyone, or every sponsor will lobby next.`,
                    hint_diluted:           `Your coaching is spread too thin. Six sessions is the depth that changes behaviour; concentrate it on a priority few and extend reach with peer circles instead.`,
                    hint_satisfaction:      `Your read-out is happy-sheets and self-report. Shift to observable behaviour on live trade-offs, rated by managers and re-assessed against the 2.4 baseline.`,
                    hint_time:              `The time-off-the-job load is above what the transformation tolerates. Lean on action-learning (on-the-job) over heavy off-site workshops and the AMP.`,
                    hint_readiness:         `Readiness is lagging with little time left. Weight the design toward challenging experience and depth coaching, and define a behaviour-based read-out the board can trust.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — readiness at target and the board credible. Lock in a transfer-based read-out and an embedding mechanism to hold it past month 18.`,

